y habits to prevent and manage migraine \nShared by Dr. Sindhuja  |  Anesthesiologist, Interventional Pain & Regenerative Specialist \nMigraine is more than \"just a headache\". It often comes with throbbing pain, nausea, and sensitivity to light and \nsound. The good news: small, steady lifestyle habits can reduce how often it strikes. Here is your quick guide. \n1 \nStay Hydrated \n• Sip water regularly through \nthe day \n• Avoid long periods without \nfluids \n• Drink more in hot weather or \nexercise \n• Carry a water bottle with you \n \n2 \nDon\'t Skip Meals \n• Keep regular meal timings \n• Avoid long gaps between \nmeals \n• Keep a healthy snack handy \n• Do not start the day on an \nempty stomach \n \n3 \nMaintain Good Sleep \n• Fixed sleep and wake-up time \n• Avoid frequently changing \nyour schedule \n• Give your body enough rest \n• Switch off screens before bed \n \n4 Know Your Personal Triggers \n \nKeep a simple migraine diary and note down: \n• What you ate \n• Water intake \n• Sleep duration \n• Stress level \n• Screen exposure \n• Time headache started \n• How long it lasted \nDate Food / Water Sleep (hrs) Stress (1-5) Started / Lasted \n          \n          \n \nCommon triggers to watch for: \nDehydration Skipped meals Poor / irregular sleep \nStress & anxiety Long screen time Bright light / loud noise \nStrong smells Too much caffeine Weather change \nHormonal changes (in women)   \n \n5 What To Do During an Attack \n \nRest Lie down in a quiet, dark room if light or sound worsens symptoms \nHydrate Sip water slowly and stay hydrated \nMedicine Follow only the plan prescribed by your doctor \nAvoid triggers Stay away from your known personal triggers\n\nGO TO EMERGENCY IMMEDIATELY IF YOU HAVE \n• Sudden, very severe \"worst ever\" headache \n• Weakness, numbness, or trouble speaking \n• Fever with neck stiffness \n• Sudden vision loss, confusion, or fainting \n• Headache after a head injury \n \nHelpful habits \n• Practise relaxation: deep breathing, yoga, \nmeditation \n• Take screen breaks (every 20-30 minutes) \n• Gentle regular exercise like walking \n• Limit caffeine, and have it at fixed times \n Try to avoid \n• Skipping breakfast or lunch \n• Late-night scrolling and irregular sleep \n• Self-medicating with painkillers again and again \n• Ignoring early warning signs (aura, nausea, light \nsensitivity) \n \n6 When To See a Doctor \n \nSeek medical evaluation if your migraines are: \n• Becoming more frequent \n• Becoming more severe \n• Interfering with your daily activities \n• Needing painkillers more than 2-3 days a week (overuse can worsen headaches) \n \nNeed personal advice? Consult the doctor. \nDr. Sindhuja \nInterventional Pain & Regenerative Specialist \nPhone: 9361366109 \nPalaniappa Hospital, Ramapuram, Chennai \nImportant: Migraine triggers differ from person to person. This guide is for general education and does not replace a medical consultation.','2026-11-25 07:00:01','2026-11-03 07:03:44','READY','MIGRAINE CARE GUIDE \nSimple daily habits to prevent and manage migraine \nShared by Dr. Sindhuja | Anesthesiologist, Interventional Pain & Regenerative Specialist \nMigraine is more than \"just a headache\". It often comes with throbbing pain, nausea, and sensitivity to light and \nsound.','[\"Avoid\",\"Migraine\",\"Keep\",\"Sleep\",\"Stress\"]','[\"MIGRAINE CARE GUIDE \\nSimple daily habits to prevent and manage migraine \\nShared by Dr.\",\"Sindhuja | Anesthesiologist, Interventional Pain & Regenerative Specialist \\nMigraine is more than \\\"just a headache\\\".\",\"1 \\nStay Hydrated \\n• Sip water regularly through \\nthe day \\n• Avoid long periods without \\nfluids \\n• Drink more in hot weather or \\nexercise \\n• Carry a water bottle with you\",\"2 \\nDon\'t Skip Meals \\n• Keep regular meal timings \\n• Avoid long gaps between \\nmeals \\n• Keep a healthy snack handy \\n• Do not start the day on an \\nempty stomach\",\"3 \\nMaintain Good Sleep \\n• Fixed sleep and wake-up time \\n• Avoid frequently changing \\nyour schedule \\n• Give your body enough rest \\n• Switch off screens before bed\",\"Helpful habits \\n• Practise relaxation: deep breathing, yoga, \\nmeditation \\n• Take screen breaks (every 20-30 minutes) \\n• Gentle regular exercise like walking \\n• Limit caffeine, and have it at fixed times\",\"Seek medical evaluation if your migraines are: \\n• Becoming more frequent \\n• Becoming more severe \\n• Interfering with your daily activities \\n• Needing painkillers more than 2-3 days a week (overuse can worsen headaches)\",\"Sindhuja \\nInterventional Pain & Regenerative Specialist \\nPhone: 9361366109 \\nPalaniappa Hospital, Ramapuram, Chennai \\nImportant: Migraine triggers differ from person to person.\"]','[\"4 Know Your Personal Triggers\",\"Keep a simple migraine diary and note down: \\n• What you ate \\n• Water intake \\n• Sleep duration \\n• Stress level \\n• Screen exposure \\n• Time headache started \\n• How long it lasted \\nDate Food \\/ Water Sleep (hrs) Stress (1-5) Started \\/ Lasted\",\"Common triggers to watch for: \\nDehydration Skipped meals Poor \\/ irregular sleep \\nStress & anxiety Long screen time Bright light \\/ loud noise \\nStrong smells Too much caffeine Weather change \\nHormonal changes (in women)\",\"Rest Lie down in a quiet, dark room if light or sound worsens symptoms \\nHydrate Sip water slowly and stay hydrated \\nMedicine Follow only the plan prescribed by your doctor \\nAvoid triggers Stay away from your known personal triggers\",\"GO TO EMERGENCY IMMEDIATELY IF YOU HAVE \\n• Sudden, very severe \\\"worst ever\\\" headache \\n• Weakness, numbness, or trouble speaking \\n• Fever with neck stiffness \\n• Sudden vision loss, confusion, or fainting \\n• Headache after a head injury\",\"Try to avoid \\n• Skipping breakfast or lunch \\n• Late-night scrolling and irregular sleep \\n• Self-medicating with painkillers again and again \\n• Ignoring early warning signs (aura, nausea, light \\nsensitivi